import java.util.*;
class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
    Atm a= new myatm();
        a.withdraw();
    }     
}
abstract class Atm
{
    abstract void withdraw();
}
class myatm extends Atm 
{
    void withdraw()
    {
   System.out.print("withdraw successfull");    
    }
}

explaination:-
Abstraction is one of the four pillars of object-oriented programming. 
It hides implementation details and shows only the essential functionality to the user. 
In Java, we can achieve abstraction using abstract classes and interfaces. 
For example, in an ATM, users can withdraw cash without knowing the internal transaction process. 
In my program, I use an abstract ATM class and implement the withdraw method in the child class.
